WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.759
There are many tales of people achieving all
sorts of fortune and fame by selling their

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:06.440
souls to the devil.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.930
Blues guitarist Robert Johnson is probably
the most famous subject of that tall tale.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:16.740
The legend goes that he offered his soul at
a Mississippi Delta crossroads, and in return

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.359
he would receive musical success and talents
beyond his wildest dreams.

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:26.909
And, yes, to this day he is considered a pioneer
of American blues guitar and songwriting.

00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:29.449
But the devil is in the details.

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:35.350
And with a life so shrouded in mystery, it’s
hard to separate fact from his very appealing

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.660
fiction.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:48.280
Humble Beginnings

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:54.150
Robert Johnson was born in Hazlehurst, Mississippi
on May 8, 1911, spending much of his beginnings

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.430
on a plantation in the Delta.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.120
His mother, Julia Major Dodds, was a daughter
of slaves.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.180
Dodds had ten children with her husband, but
Robert was born out of an affair with a field

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.100
hand named Noah Johnson.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.780
Later in Robert’s childhood, Julia remarried
and moved the family to Robinsonville, Mississippi.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.100
Robert seemed to take to music quickly as
a child, demonstrating keen musical ability

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.860
both on the jaw harp and harmonica.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.390
That interest didn’t fade as he grew into
his teens, as he was absent from school more

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.610
and more, which his friends attributed to
him studying music in Memphis.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.200
He would also play around with a makeshift
instrument called a diddley bow, which was

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.620
a wire attached to a house that a person could
pull taut and hit with a stick, altering the

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.080
pitch with a bottle that slides along the
wire.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.000
The early and teenage years of Robert Johnson
are very much shrouded in mystery.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.440
Historians have had to lean on anecdotal tales
from other musicians and friends of his from

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.740
those days, which is a lot like playing a
game of telephone.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.740
There just isn’t much to go on, and his
Johnson’s nomadic nature made it even harder

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.350
to get a sense of his beginnings.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:03.100
He’d pester every local musician he could
for a chance to share a stage of a jam session

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.100
with them.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.820
Many times, between other musicians’ sets,
Robert would take over the stage and play

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.000
some songs of his own.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.140
A bold move for sure.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:16.110
Legendary bluesman Son House knew Robert Johnson
during these years, and wasn’t exactly blown

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.500
away by the young man’s ability at the time:

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.280
"Folks they come and say, 'Why don't you go
out and make that boy put that thing down?

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.460
He running us crazy,' " House said.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.460
"Finally he left.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:32.410
He run off from his mother and father, and
went over in Arkansas some place or other."

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:37.390
When he was 18, Robert married sixteen-year-old
Virginia Travis, but tragedy struck soon after,

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.250
as she died during childbirth.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:44.170
Rumors abounded that it was punishment for
his burgeoning musician lifestyle, but he

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:48.160
forged ahead, deciding to dedicate his life
to becoming a bluesman.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.760
He did manage to marry again in 1931, at the
age of 20, though she too died while giving

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:54.760
birth.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.410
This set him on an even more solitary path.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.819
Johnson would stay with numerous women, whom
he would return to from time to time, but

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:04.770
he remained single, adopting different aliases.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:08.989
The women he frequented mostly didn’t know
about each other, and he preferred it that

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:09.989
way.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.580
He was a loner, committed to playing music
and travelling the road, playing music at

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.200
his frequent stops.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.720
At his shows he would play mostly crowd-pleasing
songs of the time, including jazz and country

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:19.720
standards.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.050
And even though he would form strong temporary
relationships with the townsfolk and local

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:30.150
musicians, his stays would be brief, and then
he would move on as easily as the breeze.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:35.810
Birth of the Legend

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.840
Son House mentioned that Johnson went to Arkansas
for a time, and was somehow very different

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.310
when he returned to Mississippi.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:46.860
The prevailing thought was that Johnson at
the time was a mediocre guitar player, but

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.940
overnight seemed to have gained an almost
supernatural ability at the instrument.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.297
That's where the myth came about that he sold
his soul to the devil late one night at a

00:03:53.297 --> 00:03:55.610
Delta crossroads, in order to become a virtuosic
guitarist and songwriter.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:56.780
It's the only possible explanation, right?

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.909
Pop culture has had a long love affair with
the idea of a man selling his existence to

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:03.360
an evil entity for musical riches.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.129
The 1986 film Crossroads had leading man Ralph
Macchio on a countrywide quest to learn about

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:14.739
the legend of that devilish pact, and to find
a lost song that Robert Johnson never got

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:16.340
to show to the world.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.799
2000’s O Brother, Where Art Thou saw the
trio of prison fugitives led by George Clooney

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:24.630
coming across one Tommy Johnson, who, you
guessed it, sold his soul to Satan to be able

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:25.630
to play the guitar.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:31.090
It’s a fantastical story, and that’s why
it’s been told for so long, and in so many

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.090
mediums.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:36.000
And of course it’s fiction, but why let
the truth get in the way of a good story?

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.430
What’s more likely is that Robert Johnson
practiced tirelessly at home, and at any clubs

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.310
that would give him an opportunity to play.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:46.840
And he even allegedly admitted to people that
during his time away, he had been studying

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.889
with some kind of guitar teacher.

00:04:48.889 --> 00:04:52.940
But it seemed like he had taken those lessons
to some kind of new heights.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.229
His technique was something almost completely
new, in which some of his guitar lines mimicked

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:59.760
what the bass parts of a piano would play.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.260
In fact, many who studied his playing noticed
that he did some kind of impressive combination

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.010
of playing slide, rhythm, and bass all at
the same time, while also singing.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:12.410
Playing non-stop is where those chops came
from.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:19.090
Creating A Lasting Impression

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:23.320
Those chops came in handy, as Johnson in 1931
was gaining enough traction to be able to

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.220
play lots and lots of gigs.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:30.020
For most of the 1930s, Robert Johnson moved
from his Mississippi Delta stomping grounds,

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:35.130
to Memphis and Arkansas, busking on street
corners with his acoustic guitar for tips.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:39.520
When other musicians would offer him gigs,
he’d travel to New York, Chicago, and many

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.180
other cities around the country.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.750
Johnny Shines was one of the musicians he
would often share bills with during his touring.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:50.380
Turns out Shines is one of the sole sources
of information that we have about Robert Johnson

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.770
during those years.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:55.770
Shines met Johnson through a mysterious piano
player up in Memphis, and even though the

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.750
two didn’t quite hit it off, eventually
a friendship was formed.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:02.360
The two would hit a new town and set up on
opposing street corners, playing for tips.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.860
They both had a wandering spirit, Shines remembered:

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.660
“Robert was a guy, you could wake him up
anytime, and he was ready to go.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.160
You say ‘Robert I here and train.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.610
Let’s catch it,’ and he wouldn’t exchange
no words with you.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:14.760
He was ready to go.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.720
It didn’t make no difference (where).

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.640
Just so he was going.”

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.280
Every single gathering you could imagine in
the American South where you could enjoy music,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.560
Johnson and Shines would entertain at.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.870
House parties, soul food dinners, even coal
yard and sawmills, the two would play music

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.500
for the people gathered there.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:37.430
They encountered some hairy moments at a stop
in Arkansas, where Shines’ cousin got into

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.630
a shooting match with his father-in-law.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.510
Johnson and Shines took that as an omen, and
spent a few months in the northern part of

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:45.030
the country, near Detroit.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:50.400
But Robert felt the call of the Mississippi
Delta deeply, and always returned back home.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.030
Robert Johnson did seek out a more permanent
partner in 1936, but this was in the form

00:06:56.030 --> 00:07:00.479
of making connections so that he could finally
lay down some music in the studio.

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:04.539
He was eventually paired up with producer
Don Law, but it wasn’t a conventional studio

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:05.620
they recorded in.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.800
It was a hotel in San Antonio.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.770
Regardless of the facilities, those three
days of recordings would go down as some of

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.400
the most important sessions ever in the history
of blues and rock music.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.350
Johnson recorded in a corner of one of the
rooms, not because he was shy (as many music

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:25.330
historians thought), but to concentrate the
sound in one area to maximize the sound of

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:29.620
the guitar, which by then Johnson was quite
proficient with.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.949
Under the Influence

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:39.550
“Terraplane Blues”, “Come On In My Kitchen”,
and “Kind Hearted Woman Blues” were some

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:44.669
of the sixteen songs that Johnson laid down,
and then another session in Dallas the next

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:46.810
year netted several more tunes.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:51.410
Overall, the total output of Robert Johnson’s
recording sessions in his life was 29 songs,

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:56.570
but that small amount of music influenced
everyone from The Rolling Stones, to Muddy

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:59.229
Waters, to Led Zeppelin, to Fleetwood Mac.

00:07:59.229 --> 00:08:03.961
Almost any artist who worked within blues
arrangements (and many who did not) owes some

00:08:03.961 --> 00:08:06.350
kind of debt of gratitude to the bluesman.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:11.020
“Terraplane Blues” actually became a modest
enough hit in his own lifetime, but his legacy

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:14.050
wouldn't grow until long after his passing.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.050
Which was imminent.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:21.330
A Mysterious End

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:25.690
Just a year after his Dallas recordings, Robert
Johnson died in Greenwood, Mississippi.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:29.270
No one knows the cause, but he was a mere
27 years old at the time.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:33.289
The public and the press had no idea he had
passed away, and it wasn’t until thirty

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:36.709
years later that a historian happened upon
his death certificate.

00:08:36.709 --> 00:08:41.529
He was simply found on the side of a country
road, with no potential motives or reasons

00:08:41.529 --> 00:08:42.769
for his passing.

00:08:42.769 --> 00:08:47.089
Some say it was a jealous husband who murdered
him after Johnson flirted with his wife.

00:08:47.089 --> 00:08:49.329
Other accounts say that he was poisoned.

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:53.369
In more recent years, some medical professionals
floated the theory that it could have been

00:08:53.369 --> 00:08:54.369
syphilis.

00:08:54.369 --> 00:08:57.920
In fact, the back of his death certificate
has a handwritten note stating the disease

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.610
as his cause of death.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:03.870
Which, with his proclivity for women, wouldn’t
be far out of the realm of possibility.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:08.629
But like so much of his legend, no one really
knows how to fill in the gaps.

00:09:08.629 --> 00:09:11.310
And no one really knows where he’s buried.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.740
The timing of his death could not have been
worse.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:19.170
Johnson was just about to be showcased at
Carnegie Hall for a monumental tribute concert.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:24.259
His meager success so far with his songs would
undoubtedly have skyrocketed after such a

00:09:24.259 --> 00:09:25.399
high-profile event.

00:09:25.399 --> 00:09:30.300
Iconic Columbia Records executive John Hammond
had Johnson in mind specifically to lend his

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:34.779
talents to the event, but sadly it was not
to be.

00:09:34.779 --> 00:09:37.870
The Legacy

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:45.380
Ironically, decades later, John Hammond would
put together the album reissue of Robert Johnson’s

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:50.420
songs that would finally make him a household
name as a father of blues music.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.820
1961’s King of the Delta Blues Singers might
not have made the splash that it did without

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:56.300
some serendipitous events.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:01.970
But Hammond took one of those freshly-pressed
LPs in 1961 and handed it to Bob Dylan.

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.149
Dylan was immediately enthralled, and even
featured the cover of Johnson’s record on

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:07.779
one of his own album covers.

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:13.040
Soon after, Johnson’s album would be the
barometer of how hip somebody was in the 60s.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:16.650
Eric Clapton would take the torch and run
with it too, playing some of his music in

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:21.449
Cream and then devoting an entire album to
Johnson later in his solo career.

00:10:21.449 --> 00:10:25.329
Strangely, if white rock musicians hadn’t
copped many of his songs when rock music was

00:10:25.329 --> 00:10:29.069
exploding in the 1960s, we may have never
heard of Robert Johnson.

00:10:29.069 --> 00:10:35.110
Again, it cannot be overstated just how unknown
Robert Johnson was at the time of his death

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:38.649
and long after, outside of serious music collectors.

00:10:38.649 --> 00:10:42.540
Not only was no one really aware of how he
died, or where he was interred, but most people

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.939
just didn’t even know that he was a thing.

00:10:44.939 --> 00:10:50.600
Until the 1960s, Robert Johnson was on very
few peoples’ radar, as far as his talent

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.040
and the things he contributed to in the music
world.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:58.149
In the 1990s, Johnson’s legacy received
another shot in the arm with the release of

00:10:58.149 --> 00:10:59.220
The Complete Recordings.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.220
Which, didn’t the world already have all
29 of his songs?

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:04.019
Anyway, the reissue sold a few million copies
and won a Grammy award for best historical

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:07.489
record, shining a light on his accomplishments
all over again.

00:11:07.489 --> 00:11:12.009
Some artists wish they could build a body
of work over decades, releasing album after

00:11:12.009 --> 00:11:13.819
album of consistent material.

00:11:13.819 --> 00:11:17.519
Robert Johnson did that in two recording sessions.

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:21.059
A couple of days worth of work that would
change the guitar and music in a way that’s

00:11:21.059 --> 00:11:22.939
still not really credited properly.

00:11:22.939 --> 00:11:26.679
The fact that he also had devil stories and
crossroads and stories of selling souls only

00:11:26.679 --> 00:11:27.889
enhances his mythos.

00:11:27.889 --> 00:11:33.889
It’s like a creepy icing on the cake, but
it in no way diminishes the quality and urgency

00:11:33.889 --> 00:11:34.889
of his music.

00:11:34.889 --> 00:11:48.800
That is plenty devilish by itself.

